CAJOLERY The future of a community or a nation can be measurably predicted upon the mental, moral and spiritual stamina of its youth this fact is recognized and conceded by the opponents of the majority of this Territory.

It is felt by such, that ordinary and probably extraordinary effort could accomplish but little among those of mature years, or of large experience in so-called "Mormonism;"but that any hope for future disintegration of this system must come from perversion of the youth.

It is readily understood that there is pliability in inexperience, and to take advantage of this fact is no discredit, providing, the inculcation and practical application of primal or advanced truth, is really the object desired.

But the youth of Utah occupy an anomalous position in the commonwealth; anywhere else, religionists are expected to train their own posterity;

and deliberate encroachment upon this established practice would not be brooked by any party;

but there are those in Utah who deliberately counsel disobedience to parental authority, who hold up to ridicule, those honored as teachers, who falsify facts and pervert, persistently, the intent and meaning of most sacred things, in order to create distrust and relegate to contempt or indifference the faith, friends and wishes of their fathers and mothers.

The youthful mind, thus unsuspectingly, imbibes thoughts and ideas which would fall as light as snowflakes upon the fathers, who, for years, have been berated and misrepresented by opponents;

it is almost incredible to our youth, that men should so continuously falsify, and through the press, so repeatedly resurrect the proven inconsistencies and myths of the years gone by!

But of late there is a "new departure''in the camp of the enemy, raillery and ridicule being unsuccessful, it is proposed to win by inflaming the ambition of our youth; they are kindly reminded they are "out of harmony with the age,"that "slavery and bondage are in the AND FLATTERY.

Church,"that liberty and freedom are all outside;

that they must, under the "restraints of the Priesthood,"be "hewers of wood and drawers of water,"but that in the assertion of themselves, their rights and privileges etc., all the avenues of preferment, emolument and honor are open to their will. So there is an appearance of interest, of regard, a disposition to patronize, to assist, to advance in life all who can by this process in any way be lured or cajoled!

Reflection and experience sees in this however the old tempter, even without the trouble of disguise. Turn for a moment to "The Book:"Again the devil taketh him up into an exceeding high mountain, and showeth him all the kingdoms of this world, and the glory of them; and saith unto him, All these things will I give thee if thou wilt fall down and worship me.

Then saith Jesus unto him, Get thee hence Satan, for it is written thou shalt worship the Lord thy God and him only, shalt thou serve.—Matthew iv, 8, 9, 10.

How nearly parallel the offers of ancient and modern times;

only forsake your religion, give up your faith in God, your regard for the gospel, and be "as one of us,"and we will award you a fair share of place, perquisites and plunder;

office, political perferment government position can be all yours "if you will only pay the price!"

Those who accept the bribe will be very few indeed?

nay, there could be no calamity so great, so fatal in its effects, as to inoculate our youth with that greed for office, which already far too much is the characteristic of the majority of this Republic.

It is a more healthy sign when "office seeks the man,"than "when men seek the office,"and if these are to be bought by fealty to party, and by becoming traitorous to truth, let our youth avoid office as long as they live, for this "blood poisoning,"which men sometimes call patriotism, meaning self, will leave its effects both in time and in eternity.

There is no fear however in regard even to public honor being long withheld from our youth. We are a growing people,
